<interrogationes unitas="stml_macros">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I load an STML document so templates, queries and anonymous lists are resolved before I read it?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_legere stml_expandere stml_distribuere" via="project-specs/stml-instrumentum-spec.md tools/stml.c">Run
      the chain with ONE InternamentumChorda and one piscina:
      stml_legere, then stml_expandere(resultus.radix, ...), then
      stml_distribuere(expansio.radix_expansa, ...). Pass the
      DOCUMENT node (resultus.radix), not elementum_radix, or
      document-level doctype, processing instructions and comments
      fall outside the subtree. stml_expandere and stml_distribuere
      each return a NEW tree and leave their input untouched, so elementum_radix still points
      into the original tree afterwards and must be found again in
      the result. tools/stml.c (the stml expandere verb) is the
      reference caller; spec section 3.1 lists the laws.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I check radix_expansa != NIHIL to know that stml_expandere worked?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_expandere StmlExpansioResultus">No:
      test successus. Some vitia are soft. Anything that goes wrong
      while a reference in a string position (attribute value,
      interpolated text, an inner call's arguments) is substituted is
      recorded but does not stop the walk: a subtree argument there is
      ARGUMENTUM_ARBOREUM (VII), and a failing projection there is
      PROIECTIO_ABSENS or PROIECTIO_AMBIGUA (XXVII, XXVIII). The
      literal &amp;@p; stays in place. A relation that no PER and no
      de= consumed is EXITUS_NON_CONSUMPTUS (XVIII), set by the end
      guard. In these cases radix_expansa is a non-NIHIL but wrong
      tree. Hard vitia stop the walk and return radix_expansa NIHIL. Only the first vitium is reported,
      with linea, fragmentum and loculus filled where they apply
      (empty chorda otherwise).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does every template call fail with FRAGMENTUM_IGNOTUM even though the definition is right there?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_expandere stml_legere internamentum_creare" via="project-specs/stml-instrumentum-spec.md">Most
      likely stml_expandere got a different InternamentumChorda from
      the one stml_legere used. Definitions keep the id the parser
      interned, calls intern the id with the intern you pass, and the
      two are compared by pointer, so with a second intern no call
      ever finds its definition. Create one intern before stml_legere
      and pass it to every stage.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the difference between a #f fragment and a #@f template, and why does my call not expand?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_expandere" via="lib/stml_macros.worklog.md">Only
      ids that start with @ are templates. &lt;#@f&gt; defines one; it is
      removed from the expanded tree, and each &lt;&lt;#@f ...&gt;&gt; is
      replaced by a fresh filled copy of its body (instantiation).
      &lt;#f&gt; and &lt;&lt;#f&gt;&gt; are content fragments and
      transclusions: stml_expandere copies them through untouched,
      because a plain transclusion is an alias (the same object seen
      twice), not a copy. &lt;&lt;#solus&gt;&gt; with no definition is
      therefore not an error. Decided 2026-08-26 when arbor's shared
      lexemes would otherwise have been deleted.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I write a template with parameters and pass it text or a subtree?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_expandere">Declare
      each slot as an attribute VALUE starting with @ on the defining
      tag (the attribute name is ignored: k="@p" declares p), and
      refer to it in the body as &amp;@p; in text or attribute values:
      &lt;#@f p="@p"&gt;&lt;a x="&amp;@p;"/&gt;&lt;/#&gt;. Call it inline
      with &lt;&lt;#@f p="v"&gt;&gt;, or follow the call with block arguments
      &lt;@p=&gt;...&lt;/&gt;. A block with no children is a tombstone (the
      slot counts as not passed). A block holding one text child is a
      scalar, unless that text is exactly &amp;@x; naming a subtree
      argument of the enclosing template, which passes that subtree
      on. Any other block is a subtree, which may only stand alone as
      the whole text &amp;@p; (it is spliced as a fresh copy each
      time). A subtree in an attribute or mixed text is vitium VII.
      Every required slot must be filled (IV), no unknown name may be
      passed (V), and no name twice, inline or block (VIII). Since any
      value of two or more characters starting with @ is a
      declaration, a defining tag cannot carry a literal value like
      "@home" ("@" alone and values starting "@." stay ordinary). The
      value "@?" declares a required slot named ? that no reference
      can name.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I leave a template argument out or pass it as an empty string?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_expandere chorda_internare">Leave
      it out: declare the slot optional with p="@p?", then omit it or
      pass the tombstone &lt;@p=/&gt;. Select on it with
      &lt;COMMUTATIO de="&amp;@p;"&gt; and &lt;CASUS nihil&gt; /
      &lt;CASUS non-nihil&gt; arms (or est="value"). Using &amp;@p;
      outside an arm that proves presence is vitium XI. Do NOT pass
      p="" inline: chorda_internare returns NIHIL for an empty
      chorda, so the argument has neither value nor subtree. In text
      or an attribute that dereferences NIHIL and crashes, and
      against an est arm it reports ARGUMENTUM_ARBOREUM (both seen
      2026-10-17).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does calling a template that is defined further down the document fail?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_expandere" via="lib/stml_macros.worklog.md">Strata:
      a call can only reach definitions EARLIER in document order, so
      a later one is FRAGMENTUM_POSTERIUS (II), at document level and
      inside bodies alike. Termination is then guaranteed by
      construction, because each nested fill may only use strictly
      older definitions. Definitions are collected from any depth of
      the document, inside ordinary elements too, and dropped from the
      output. A template defined INSIDE another template's body is
      not collected: calls to it are FRAGMENTUM_IGNOTUM, and each
      instantiation copies the nested definition verbatim into the
      output. The one
      exception is a template calling itself: allowed only when every
      subtree argument is a strict descendant of the current
      instantiation's subtree arguments and there is at least one
      (else RECURSIO_NON_DESCENDENS, XXXI). Mutual recursion is still
      refused.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find elements in the document and generate output for each match?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_expandere" via="project-specs/stml-exemplaria-spec.md">Use
      an EXEMPLAR to bind a relation and a PER to consume it:
      &lt;EXEMPLAR output="$a"&gt;&lt;q id="$i"/&gt;&lt;/EXEMPLAR&gt; then
      &lt;PER congruentia="$a"&gt;&lt;v n="&amp;@i;"/&gt;&lt;/PER&gt;
      emits one &lt;v&gt; per match at the PER's place. A value written
      in the pattern is required and an omitted one is free. id="$i"
      captures a value, a bare $n attribute captures the node, a text
      child of only $t captures the text, and &lt;*&gt; matches any tag.
      An EXEMPLAR searches only the content ABOVE it in the document.
      modus is omnia (the default), unum (exactly one, else XVI),
      primum or optional. Both commands are consumed and never
      emitted. They work only at document level: inside a template
      body they are vitium XIII, except PER de="@n..." over a subtree
      argument. CATENA, DIRIBITIO and SINE chain, branch on and
      filter relations.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I see what my EXEMPLAR, CATENA or PER commands actually matched?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_expandere">Put
      a bare &lt;INDAGO/&gt; at the top of the document. Every consumed
      EXEMPLAR, PER, CATENA or DIRIBITIO after it at document level
      leaves a comment in its place (TRANSPARENTIA does not), for example
      &lt;!--indago EXEMPLAR $a (radix): ordines 1 (capturae: i)--&gt;,
      with per-stage row counts for chains and taken arms for
      DIRIBITIO. The trace is a comment, so matching ignores it, but
      the output bytes do change. Commands BEFORE the element are
      not traced. INDAGO with any attribute or a non-comment child is
      vitium XXIV.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does stml_distribuere do, and do I need it if I already called stml_expandere?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_distribuere StmlDistributioResultus" via="project-specs/stml-instrumentum-spec.md">It
      dissolves list wrappers. A named element whose children (after
      any leading &lt;@x=&gt; attribute elements) are ALL anonymous
      fragments becomes one copy of itself per fragment:
      &lt;nota t="a"&gt;&lt;&gt;lac&lt;/&gt;&lt;&gt;ianuam&lt;/&gt;&lt;/nota&gt; becomes two
      &lt;nota t="a"&gt; elements. Wrapper attributes and attribute
      elements are copied into each item, and an item attribute of
      the same name wins. Mixing anonymous fragments with named children or text is
      STML_DISTRIBUTIO_MIXTA, with linea of the first offending child
      and the wrapper's titulus; comments pass through.
      Any element qualifies, the root included: &lt;r&gt;&lt;&gt;x&lt;/&gt;&lt;/r&gt;
      becomes &lt;r&gt;x&lt;/r&gt;. Treat it as required: skipping it
      silently changes how many elements there are.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I use stml_congruere_strictum as a general pattern matcher for STML trees?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_congruere_strictum StmlCaptura StmlCongruentiaPar xar_truncare">Only
      for exact shapes. Tag, fragment and raw flags must be equal,
      and the attribute lists must be equal IN THE SAME ORDER
      (x="1" y="2" does not match y="2" x="1"). Children must match
      one to one, except that a template whose only child is the
      whole text &amp;@k; captures all the candidate's children (at
      least one) into an StmlCaptura. A repeated slot must capture
      byte-identical forests. paria (may be NIHIL) gets
      StmlCongruentiaPar entries as it goes and is not rolled back on
      failure, so save xar_numerus first and xar_truncare back to it.
      capturae (must not be NIHIL) is not rolled back either: pass a
      fresh Xar for each attempt, as silva_arbor.c does.
      For subset matching (omitted attributes free, children an
      ordered subsequence), use an EXEMPLAR inside the document
      instead.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find which template call produced a node in the expanded tree?
    <responsum symbolum="StmlExpansioNota stml_expandere">Read
      tabula_expansionum, a Xar of StmlExpansioNota in splice order.
      nodus is the first node the call spliced (NIHIL if the body
      produced nothing), vocatio is the call node
      in the ORIGINAL tree (keep that tree alive while you use it),
      fragmentum_id is the interned id and stratum the 1-based nesting
      depth. PER voca="#@f" adds one entry per row. Calls inside
      block arguments are recorded before the call that receives
      them, and their nodus points into the argument's stored subtree,
      not into radix_expansa: that subtree is cloned at each place it
      is spliced, so look such a node up through the receiving call's
      entry instead.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I get a printable name for the vitium in a failed StmlExpansioResultus?
    <responsum symbolum="StmlExpansioVitium StmlDistributioVitium" via="tools/stml.c include/stml_macros.h">Not
      from the library: there is no public name function, only the
      enum (I to XXXI for expansion, MIXTA and MEMORIA for
      distribution), each value with its comment in the header.
      tools/stml.c keeps a private switch (_vitium_expansionis_titulus)
      that prints the name with linea, fragmentum and loculus; copy
      it, and keep it in step when a vitium is added.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
